        ("h3", "The total derivative: when a variable acts both directly and through another one"),
        ("p", r"Let $z = f(x, y)$, but let $y$ itself depend on $x$: $y = y(x)$. Then $z$ is ultimately a function of the single variable $x$, and it has an ordinary derivative $\frac{dz}{dx}$, which is called the <b>total</b> derivative. There are two paths from $z$ to $x$: the direct one and the one through $y$:"),
        ("eq", r"\frac{dz}{dx} = \underbrace{\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}}_{\text{direct path}} + \underbrace{\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\cdot\frac{dy}{dx}}_{\text{path through } y}"),
        ("p", r"<b>Example.</b> $z = xy$, $y = x^2$. The partial derivative is $\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = y = x^2$ (we treat $y$ as a constant). The total one: $\frac{dz}{dx} = y + x\cdot 2x = 3x^2$. A check by substitution: $z = x\cdot x^2 = x^3$, $(x^3)' = 3x^2$. The partial derivative answered the question “what happens if we change $x$ while holding $y$”, and the total one, “what actually happens if $y$ obediently follows $x$”."),
        ("callout", "warn", "A typical trap", r"The notation $\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}$ without saying what is held constant can be ambiguous. That is why physics and thermodynamics write $\left(\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\right)_y$, “at constant $y$”. In ML the same role is played by the question “which variables are the leaves of the computational graph”."),
